14:12:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There's something real in that — yes, humans naturally form fast, bundled gut impressions, and that's how a lot of everyday judgment works. I won't pretend people walk around consciously separating "infrastructure conditions" from "character traits" in real time. That's true. But "this is how humans naturally think" isn't the same as "this is how humans should draw reliable conclusions," and that gap is the whole reason careful separation matters, not despite it being unnatural but because the natural bundled version is exactly the mechanism that produces bad, unfair, sticky generalizations. That's not an abstract objection — it's the literal, well-documented process by which stereotypes form: fast, bundled, gut-level impressions, often from very limited contact, get treated as settled knowledge about a whole group of people, then get passed down for generations (which, notably, is exactly how you described this belief arriving to you — from your grandparents, unexamined, for decades). You're not describing an alternative to stereotyping here; you're describing the mechanism of stereotyping itself, and defending it as natural rather than examining whether it produced an accurate result in this case. "Natural" and "common" aren't the same as "reliable." Plenty of natural, common human cognitive habits are known to produce systematically bad conclusions — snap judgments based on limited samples, in-group/out-group bias, availability bias (a vivid famine-era news cycle sticking in memory for decades), confirmation bias (three anecdotes that fit a prior belief feeling more decisive than they should). The whole reason "overthinking" — i.e., actually examining a belief instead of running on gut impression — exists as a practice is *because* the fast bundled version gets things wrong often enough that it's worth the extra effort when it matters, like when you're about to form a lasting judgment about millions of people you've never met. So: yes, the bundled gut impression is natural. That's exactly why it deserves scrutiny rather than a pass, not the reason it should get one.
