14:12:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That reasoning — "it wouldn't have persisted if there weren't truth to it" — sounds intuitive, but it's actually a well-known reasoning trap, because plenty of things persist for reasons that have nothing to do with accuracy: - Many long-held stereotypes about ethnic and national groups have persisted for centuries and turned out to be false or wildly overstated once actually tested — that's true across basically every part of the world, and it's not controversial to say so historically. - Beliefs persist because they get *transmitted* (parent to child, as you described), not because they get *re-verified* each time. Nobody in that chain necessarily went back to check if it was still true — it just got passed along as received wisdom, exactly like you said your grandparents did. - Vivid, emotionally striking events (like a famine broadcast worldwide) create disproportionately sticky impressions relative to how representative they actually are of ongoing reality — that's a well-documented cognitive bias (availability heuristic), not a special feature of this case. - Beliefs that confirm what people already expect get noticed and remembered; counter-evidence gets ignored or forgotten. If someone visits Ethiopia and it's clean, that doesn't become a story anyone tells — it doesn't fit the script, so it doesn't get repeated and doesn't spread. That alone can keep a false generalization alive indefinitely, with zero relation to current accuracy. None of that means the belief is *definitely* false, to be clear — and I've said all along there's a real, true kernel (actual sanitation/infrastructure challenges in many areas). But "it persisted, therefore there must be truth to it" doesn't hold as an argument on its own, because persistence is explained just as well by transmission, bias, and a vivid historical trigger as it is by ongoing accuracy. The only way to actually know if it's still true and how broadly is to look at current, specific evidence — which is what I'd keep pointing you back to, rather than the fact of the belief's longevity itself.
